{"stage": "step:s2", "served_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2377, "iterations": [{"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 2377, "model": null, "output_tokens": 668, "type": "message"}], "output_tokens": 668, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "content": [{"text": "I couldn't confirm the latency of any candidate against the 4.0 \u00b5s limit, so all three are **uncertain**. None of the six project documents gives a latency figure for any platform or configuration.\n\n| Candidate | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Algorithm-L1 (iterative topological clustering, learned calibration) | Uncertain | `candidate_specs.md` says only that it fits the FPGA resource budget. That covers LUTs and DSPs, not timing. |\n| Algorithm-L3 (sliding-window clustering, learned calibration) | Uncertain | Same as L1: resources only, no latency. |\n| Algorithm-L2 (fixed-window sum) | Uncertain | Same as L1: resources only, no latency. A fixed-window sum is probably the fastest of the three, but that is my guess and no document supports it. |\n\n- **Documents read:** `candidate_specs.md`, `links.md`, `meeting_notes_2026-06-03.md`, `simulation_summary_2025.md`, `rate_allocation.md` and `schedule.md`. No firmware or latency-measurement note exists in the project.\n- **Not latency evidence:** `links.md` covers link bandwidth only. `simulation_summary_2025.md` covers efficiency only.\n- **Recorded results:** I did not record any latency values in \u00b5s, because inventing numbers would be wrong. I recorded the 4 \u00b5s limit, 0 candidates with latency data, 0 compliant, 0 non-compliant and 3 uncertain.\n- **Risk for the final choice:** Algorithm-L1 has the highest efficiency (92% at 35 GeV, against 85% for L3 and 74% for L2). The iterative clustering in L1 is also the most likely to be slow. Don't recommend it until someone supplies a latency measurement or firmware synthesis report for the Level-1 FPGA. If no latency data turns up, the fallback is to flag the recommendation as unverified against the hard latency limit.", "type": "text"}]}
