{"stage": "step:s3", "served_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 5171, "iterations": [{"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 5171, "model": null, "output_tokens": 595, "type": "message"}], "output_tokens": 595, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "content": [{"text": "I ran the efficiency scan for all three algorithms and recorded the results. I scanned all three because no latency data exists yet, so none can be called compliant. Each algorithm peaks between 30 and 40 GeV, and Algorithm-L1 is the most efficient at every threshold.\n\nSignal efficiency (%) at the allocated 8 kHz rate:\n\n| Threshold (GeV) | Algorithm-L1 | Algorithm-L2 | Algorithm-L3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 20 | 86.7 | 69.3 | 79.8 |\n| 30 | 91.1 | 74.8 | 84.2 |\n| 35 | 91.9 | 74.3 | 85.1 |\n| 40 | 92.1 | 74.3 | 83.8 |\n| 50 | 88.0 | 70.3 | 80.9 |\n| 60 | 77.0 | 63.1 | 70.5 |\n\n- **Best thresholds:** Algorithm-L1 peaks at 40 GeV (92.1%), Algorithm-L2 at 30 GeV (74.8%) and Algorithm-L3 at 35 GeV (85.1%).\n- **Refinement:** I added a 35 GeV point for each algorithm to refine around the peaks. I did not scan finer than that. Algorithm-L1 is nearly flat between 35 and 40 GeV (91.9% and 92.1%), and Algorithm-L2 is nearly flat across 30\u201340 GeV, so their peak positions are not well determined.\n- **Latency:** The 4.0 \u00b5s hard limit is still unchecked for every candidate, so the final choice depends on getting latency numbers. If Algorithm-L1 fails the limit, Algorithm-L3 is the next best option.", "type": "text"}]}
